[' Here is a simple C function that implements the described behavior using `fgets()` function instead of `scanf()`, as `scanf()` does not support reading a line directly into a buffer without including the newline character.

```c
#include <stdio.h>
#include <string.h>

size_t scan(char* buffer, size_t max_length) {
    if (max_length > 1024) {
        fprintf(stderr, "Buffer size cannot exceed 1023 bytes\n");
        return 0;
    }

    // fgets reads until newline or end of file and stores the newline in buffer. We remove it afterward.
    if (fgets(buffer, max_length + 1, stdin) == NULL) {
        perror("Error reading from standard input");
        return 0;
    }

    // Remove the newline character at the end of the string if present.
    size_t length = strlen(buffer);
    if (length > 0 && buffer[length - 1] == '\n') {
        buffer[length - 1] = '\0';
        --length;
    }

    return length;
}
```

This function takes a character array and the maximum size of that array as input. It ensures the maximum size doesn't exceed 1024 bytes, reads in a line using `fgets()`, removes the newline character if present, and returns the number of characters read.']

